# How to know which version of Symfony I have?

Determining the exact version of a Symfony project can be tricky if you've been updating your `vendor` directory without a strict version-locking strategy. Unlike static binaries where the path is obvious, a modern PHP framework lives inside a dynamic dependency graph. When you run `composer update`, Composer resolves dependencies against `composer.lock`, often pulling in minor or patch updates that change the underlying Symfony core without necessarily changing the version number displayed in your root composer file. If you simply look at `composer.json` and see `"symfony/symfony": "5.*"`, you are looking at a semver range, not a specific release. You need to dig into the actual installed packages to get the truth.

The most reliable method to check the version of the main Symfony framework is to inspect the `vendor/composer` directory or use the Composer CLI directly. The standard approach involves checking the `installed.json` file generated by Composer, which acts as a cached snapshot of all resolved dependencies. You can query this file using a simple command line operation:

```bash
cat vendor/composer/installed.json | jq '.packages["symfony/symfony"].version'
```

If you don't have `jq` installed on your machine, you can still extract the version string using standard Unix tools or by leveraging PHP directly. Since Symfony is a PHP framework, running a script that uses Composer's autoloader is often faster and less prone to parsing errors than trying to parse JSON manually. Create a temporary file named `check_version.php` in your project root:

```php
<?php

require 'vendor/autoload.php';

use Symfony\Component\HttpKernel\Kernel;

// This works for projects that are currently bootstrapped as a kernel,
// but often the main framework version is distinct from the Kernel bundle.
// A more universal way without bootstrapping the app:

$packages = json_decode(file_get_contents('vendor/composer/installed.json'), true);

if (isset($packages['symfony/symfony'])) {
    echo "Symfony Framework Version: " . $packages['symfony/symfony']['version'] . PHP_EOL;
} else {
    // Fallback for monolithic Symfony 2 or specific setups where symfony/framework-bundle is the main entry
    foreach ($packages as $key => $value) {
        if (stripos($key, 'symfony/') === 0 && stripos($key, 'framework') !== false) {
            echo "Found Symfony package: {$key} - Version: {$value['version']}" . PHP_EOL;
        }
    }
}
```

Running this script will output the exact version string that your application is currently using at runtime. This is crucial because `composer.json` might specify `"^5.4"`, but your actual environment could be running 5.4.3 or 5.4.9 depending on when you last ran `update`.

However, there is a significant distinction between the "Symfony Framework" and the specific bundles that make up modern applications. In Symfony 3 and later, the monolithic `symfony/symfony` package was split into individual components like `symfony/framework-bundle`, `symfony/http-kernel`, etc. If you are on a modern version, checking for `symfony/symfony` might return null. Instead, you should look for `symfony/framework-bundle` or `symfony/console`. The framework version is usually derived from the highest version of its core bundles.

For legacy projects like Symfony 2, which your source material mentions, the architecture was monolithic. In those cases, the `symfony/symfony` package was indeed the single point of reference. If you are maintaining an old codebase and trying to ensure compatibility with a specific library that requires "Symfony 2.8", checking the version of the main bundle is your only bet.

A common pitfall developers face is assuming that upgrading `phpstan/phpstan` or other tools automatically upgrades the framework. Tools like `symfony-diagram` or visualizers can also help identify versions by inspecting the class hierarchy, but they require running within the application context.

If you are considering migrating from Symfony 2 to a newer version, knowing your current exact version is the first step in planning the upgrade path. Symfony 2 reached end-of-life years ago, and many modern bundles no longer support it. If you are evaluating whether to stay on a legacy system or move forward, this version check informs your risk assessment.

For teams managing multiple environments, storing the resolved version in a CI/CD artifact or logging it at application startup (e.g., `Symfony\Component\HttpKernel\Debug::enable()` often dumps the framework info) can provide an audit trail. This ensures that when a production incident occurs, you know exactly which revision of the core logic was running.

If you need further assistance with version management or legacy Symfony upgrades, resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) often cover migration paths and modernization strategies for PHP ecosystems.
